# ARM  Istio
#
# Ten repeat runs of this sweep reduced to mean +/- sd of the pod fleet, one row
# per RPS step, one file per arm.
# Written by cld_pool_sn_pods.py; read that file's docstring for the why.
#
# SOURCE     the <run-dir>/plot_sn_pod_growth.dat files beside this one -- the same
#            settled/peak numbers plot_sn_pods.py already derived from
#            <run-dir>/<arm>/pods-<rps>-sum.csv, where a pod counts only when it
#            is BOTH phase=Running AND ready=True. This file re-reads those .dat
#            files rather than the raw CSVs, so a row here is exactly the ten
#            per-run rows averaged, with no second derivation that could drift
#            from them.
# TRANSFORM  MEAN ACROSS RUNS, not a sum: pod counts are intensive, so ten runs
#            measure the same fleet size ten times and the estimator is their
#            mean. (cld_pool_sn_latency.py sums its request counts because those
#            are extensive -- the two files merge differently on purpose.)
#            *_sd is the SAMPLE standard deviation (n-1) of those same ten
#            values: the run-to-run spread of an HPA feedback loop settling on a
#            noisy signal. It is blank where only one run measured the step,
#            because a single observation has no spread to report.
#            settled = the fleet each run ENDED the step with; peak = the largest
#            fleet each run reached during it. Both are averaged independently,
#            so settled_mean <= peak_mean but the pair no longer belongs to any
#            single run.
# CAVEAT     read settled_mean next to settled_sd, never on its own -- a gap
#            between the two arms at one step means nothing until it is larger
#            than the spread of the runs behind it. n_runs says how many runs are
#            actually under each mean.
# UNITS      ready pods, summed over every service in the chart.
# LAYOUT     one row per RPS step; one file per arm
#            (plot_sn_pod_growth_pooled_<arm>.dat). The arm is named in the
#            header block above, and the files are otherwise identical in shape.
# rps	settled_mean	settled_sd	peak_mean	peak_sd	n_runs
100	31.90	0.74	31.90	0.74	10
200	39.30	1.16	39.30	1.16	10
400	56.40	0.97	56.40	0.97	10
600	71.40	0.70	71.40	0.70	10
800	88.90	1.91	88.90	1.91	10
1000	93.90	1.66	93.90	1.66	10
1200	94.40	2.55	94.40	2.55	10
